@startuml alert-domain-model
title Spottrack — "Alert" Domain Model (currently misplaced under shared.*)

skinparam classAttributeIconSize 0
skinparam packageStyle rectangle

note as Misplacement
  Alert is a genuinely self-contained mini bounded context (its own
  aggregate, repository, command/query services, REST controller —
  see AlertsController) yet it physically lives under the
  com.spottrack.platform.shared package alongside the true shared
  kernel (Money, AbstractDomainAggregateRoot, Result, ApplicationError).
  It is NOT reused by any other bounded context — nothing about it is
  "shared". Leaving it here is exactly how a shared-kernel package
  starts turning into a dumping ground / big ball of mud. Recommended
  fix: extract it into its own top-level "alert" bounded context
  package, mirroring gym/iam/profiles/etc.
end note

package "Shared Kernel (see shared-kernel.puml)" {
  abstract class AbstractDomainAggregateRoot<T> <<shared kernel>>
}

package "Alert Aggregate" {
  class Alert <<AggregateRoot>> {
    - id: Long
    - adminUserId: Long
    - equipmentId: String
    - severity: AlertSeverity
    - message: String
    - resolved: boolean
    - createdAt: Date
    --
    + resolve()
  }

  enum AlertSeverity {
    WARNING
    CRITICAL
  }
}

package "Commands / Queries" {
  class CreateAlertCommand <<Command>> {
    adminUserId: Long
    equipmentId: String
    severity: AlertSeverity
    message: String
  }
  class ResolveAlertCommand <<Command>> {
    alertId: Long
    adminUserId: Long
  }
  class GetAlertsByAdminUserIdQuery <<Query>> {
    adminUserId: Long
  }
}

package "Repository" {
  interface AlertRepository <<Repository>> {
    + findById(Long): Optional<Alert>
    + findAllByAdminUserId(Long): List<Alert>
    + save(Alert): Alert
  }
}

Alert --|> AbstractDomainAggregateRoot
Alert *-- AlertSeverity
AlertRepository ..> Alert
CreateAlertCommand ..> AlertSeverity

note bottom of Alert
  adminUserId (iam/profiles) and equipmentId (gym) are both raw
  primitives, not typed cross-context ID VOs — same primitive-obsession
  pattern seen in membership and maintenance.
  Alert also raises no domain events; resolve() silently flips a flag
  with no MaintenanceAlertResolvedEvent-style signal for other contexts
  (e.g. analytics) to react to.
end note
@enduml
